<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anabaptism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · Also known as Anabaptists</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anabaptism is a radical Protestant movement that arose in Zürich in 1525 and spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Anabaptists rejected infant baptism, baptising only adult believers, and generally sought to separate the church from the state; Catholics and Protestants alike persecuted them. After the violent episode of Münster in 1534 to 1535, the movement turned to pacifism, surviving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> and Amish.</p></div>
